WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
"Are the Benefits of Cold Plunges
and Cryotherapy Just a Placebo Effect?"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.160
Cold-water immersion
is a post-exercise modality

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.240
purported to enhance
physical recovery

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.240
following strenuous exercise,

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.520
and people certainly report

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.040
feeling less sore,
less fatigued,

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.520
and more recovered
after cold plunging,

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.600
but the vast majority
of cryotherapy studies

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.720
have been conducted using
a do-nothing control group,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.240
like just sitting in a chair,

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.880
and have not taken
expectancy effect

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.040
or treatment belief
into account,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.880
meaning maybe the cold plunge
only works

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.880
because people think it works.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.240
In other words, could it
just be the placebo effect?

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.880
Practitioners didn’t
just feel better, though;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.480
in some cases,
they performed better too,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.920
but that also could be
a placebo effect.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.480
Give athletes placebos,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.200
and there aren’t only effects
on perceived exertion,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.480
for example,
but on actual muscle power,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.720
running speed,
and even heart rate.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.400
Placebos, like sugar pills,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.320
that are deceptively claimed

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.120
to be some
performance-enhancing drug,

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.080
actually enhance performance.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.960
Or, give one set of people
a capsule of corn starch

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.880
and say it’s going
to make them run faster,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.880
and another the same capsule

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.600
and tell them it’s going
to make them run slower,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.280
and, you guessed it,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.680
the it-will-make-you-faster
group ran faster,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.120
and the it-will-make-you-slower
group ran slower.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.640
Give people a sugar pill
and tell them

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.880
it’s a strong combination
of amino acids

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.800
with immediate effects
on strength,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.800
and they magically leg
and bench press more,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.000
until they’re informed
they were lied to

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.840
and learn
it was just a sugar pill,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:42.960
and just like that,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.800
their performance enhancement
disappeared.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.280
You can even get
a dose-response effect.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.480
When people were told
they were getting

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.920
about 300 mg of caffeine,
600 mg of caffeine,

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.120
or a placebo,

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.400
even though
everybody got placebos,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.360
the ones who were told
they got the placebo did worse,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.480
and there was increased power

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.320
in the lower
fake-caffeine group,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.600
and even more in the higher
fake-caffeine group,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.760
with all the fake-caffeinated
participants

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.640
even reporting
caffeine-related side effects.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.720
Once people are told
they are taking a substance

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.080
that will enhance performance,
all sorts of things kick in:

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.200
belief, pain sensation,
expectancy, and arousal,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.320
all of which
may impact performance.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.720
No wonder there’s a gazillion
dollar sports supplement market

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.640
selling tons of drinks,
powders, and supplements.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.960
They may work, but maybe not
any better than a sugar pill,

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.840
which would certainly be
a lot cheaper.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.280
For cold-water immersion,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.240
it’s difficult to administer
a true placebo,

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.080
but researchers cited a study

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.040
that did include
a placebo treatment,

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.480
and the effect
of cold-water immersion

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.520
was found to be no bigger
than the placebo effect.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.480
There have actually been
five such studies to date.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.400
Here’s the one
they were referring to.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.680
Thirty guys performed

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.640
an acute high-intensity
interval training session,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.560
comprised of four
30-second sprints,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.640
immediately followed
by one of the following

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.000
three 15-minute
recovery conditions:

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:10.960
cold-water immersion

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.240
at 10.3°C +/- 0.2°C, 
about 50°F,

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.240
a warm, near-body temperature
water immersion placebo,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.640
or a warm, near-body temperature
water-immersion control group.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.480
Uh, hold on.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.720
What’s the difference
between the placebo group

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.640
and the control group?

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.160
In the placebo group,

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.960
the researchers squirted
something in the water

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.840
(actually just some soap)
and led the participants

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.880
to believe it had special
recovery enhancement properties.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.400
Here’s how everyone did

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.320
in terms of recovery
of muscle strength

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.240
measuring maximal voluntary
isometric contraction strength,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.080
both peak and average.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.320
Here’s how the cold-water
immersion did,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.560
and here’s how
the warm immersion

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.280
with the fake
ergogenic soap did,

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.360
significantly better than just
the plain warm-water group.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.000
This study demonstrated
that influencing one’s belief

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.760
in the efficacy
of warm-water immersion

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.240
as an ergogenic aid
in recovery

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.920
can account for an approximate
13 percent improvement

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.080
in muscle strength 48 hours
after high-intensity exercise;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.120
so, it’s important for coaches
and sports scientists alike

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.520
to educate their athletes
on the benefits of recovery

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.280
and to also encourage belief
in the practice.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.600
Wait, are they encouraging them
to lie to athletes?

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.720
Hey, if that lie is going
to increase performance

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.640
by 13 percent.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:35.680
Here are the other four

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.320
placebo-controlled
cold-plunge studies.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.400
Cold-water immersion offers

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.120
no functional or even
perceptual benefit

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.120
compared to a sham intervention

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.520
during an eight-week
resistance training program.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.080
Here, researchers told people
in the control group

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.960
that their protein supplements
were tweaked

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.520
and that was enough
to completely eliminate

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.040
any significant effect,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.880
and had the training program
gone longer,

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.320
they would expect
the cold-immersion group

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.840
to actually shrink
long-term gains in muscle mass,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.160
or strength, or both.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.280
The next was cold-water
immersion after a soccer match,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.080
randomized to cold plunge,
passive rest recovery,

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.640
or a quote-unquote
"recovery" beverage,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.240
which was just a placebo.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.200
And both the cold plunge
and placebo were more effective

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.480
than the rest condition.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.400
Doing something made people feel
better than doing nothing,

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.240
but the cold plunge did no
better than the fake beverage,

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.600
demonstrating that indeed

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.400
cold-water immersion reduces
muscle soreness,

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.200
but this beneficial effect
was a manifestation

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.560
of the placebo effect.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.400
What about whole-body
cryotherapy--

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.280
like sitting in a cryochamber
at 100 below zero F (-73°C)--

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.000
versus cold plunge or placebo
after resistance exercise?

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.400
Neither cryotherapy intervention
was more effective

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.600
than the placebo treatment
at accelerating recovery,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.960
neither was more effective
as a pill of cornstarch,

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.200
as long as you tell people
that cornstarch pill

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.480
is a branched-chain
amino acid supplement.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.520
Oh my God, are athletes
that gullible?

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.480
No wonder these supplement
companies make off like bandits.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.640
The majority of group
comparisons revealed

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.760
unclear, trivial, or unfavorable
effects of cryotherapy

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.600
compared to the placebo
intervention,

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:18.400
meaning in some ways,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.520
the magical cornstarch pill
worked better,

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.440
contradicting much
of the previous literature,

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.840
but that’s because
much of the previous literature

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.240
compared cold-water immersion
to nothing,

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.560
just like sitting in a chair.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.600
And finally,
recovery following a marathon:

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.440
a comparison
of cold-water immersion,

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:36.720
whole-body cryotherapy,

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.160
and a placebo control group

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.280
given a fake tart cherry
juice beverage.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.520
Neither cold treatment,
whether wet or dry,

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.840
was more effective
than a placebo

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.600
at improving
functional recovery

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:51.040
or even perceptions of training
stress following a marathon.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.680
In fact, the placebo worked
even better.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.000
Cold water worked better
than the cryotherapy,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.800
but they both did worse than
the fake fruit-flavored drink.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.280
These findings lend
further evidence to suggest

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.320
that treatment belief
and the placebo effect

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:07.680
may be largely responsible

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.680
for the beneficial effects
of cryotherapy on recovery.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.200
But what about the objective
drop in muscle damage indicators

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.880
after cold-water immersion?

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.800
It’s been reasoned
that reducing

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.160
muscle temperature
and blood flow

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.320
may dampen the local
inflammatory response

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.680
and thereby boost
post-exercise recovery.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.280
What is striking is the
assumption that inflammation

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.680
needs to be dampened
or suppressed.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.760
Isn’t inflammation
a crucial component

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.600
of the adaptive response
of skeletal muscle to training?

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.280
And cold plunges aren’t
actually anti-inflammatory.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.840
Where did we even get
that idea from?

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.680
It was from studies
on lab animals

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.800
with induced
gross muscle injuries,

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.280
which isn’t exactly the same
as just exercising strenuously.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.680
Okay, but there’s still
that reduced muscle damage,

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.680
even if doesn’t affect
inflammation

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:00.080
or muscle function recovery.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.200
But doesn’t exercise-induced
muscle damage play a role

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:05.360
in skeletal muscle growth?

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.160
Might dampening the damage
be throwing cold water

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.160
on muscle growth?

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:14.036
We’ll find out next.

